      "title": "Show HN: Coldframe, a honeypot that poses as a supermarket fridge controller",
      "url": "https://github.com/hedgerow-dev/coldframe",
      "author": "hedgerow-dev",
      "external_id": "50406292",
      "summary": null,
      "why_read": null,
      "authors": [],
      "category": null,
      "published": "2026-11-07T17:27:30Z",
      "score": 257,
      "comments": 61,
      "hn_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50406292",
      "snippet": "I built Coldframe, a honeypot that pretends to be a supermarket refrigeration controller. Inspired by the events on industrial fridge installations in the past weeks, I wanted to see what people would try against a convincing fake.<p>To learn more about this I built a honeypot that resembled perfectly the Danfoss AK-SM 800A and Dixell XWEB interfaces. Both of them are recognized on Shodan as real ones (an not as honeypots). It’s one Python file using only the standard library, and it builds on eppser&#x2F;danfoss-ak-sm-decoy and public research by Synacktiv, Claroty and Ironhackers.<p>Things that were fun to get right:<p>- Matching real units. I used Shodan records of real devices to match header order, page titles, certificate fields and TLS versions. The Danfoss persona sends nginx headers in the same order as a real AK-SM 800A. The Dixell persona sends no Server header and pads its pa",
      "links": []
